## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only merging with **cause-based triage**. The merged ticket contains distinct
failure mechanisms, so fixing its majority cause will leave the other one unresolved.
Keep one engineering issue per cause, with a disjoint set of report IDs and its own acceptance criteria.

This is a proposed replacement for [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject),
not an edit to that accepted historical record. The earlier [triage guidance](/kb/triage/dedupe)
and [incident narrative](/kb/incidents/4471) are preserved as evidence of what the desk believed.
Their subject-only rule and “one issue, one fix” conclusion conflict with the executable views.
No production fix or support-policy deployment is claimed here.

<Callout kind="warn">
This analysis describes the supplied export dated 4 October. The desk keeps working: refresh
reports and tenant data before a later triage decision, then rebuild this page and reconcile the issue counts.
The review date is not the data freshness date.
</Callout>

## What the data and code show

<ReviewSummary />

### Parser: day-first input read as month-first

The shared parser assumes month/day ordering for tenants whose locale writes day/month.
For example, Harbour Dental's app reports describe April/July being exchanged.
This affects app/email paths and the relevant booking-v2 form path. A booking-v3 tenant's
app/email reports can still belong here: tenant name alone is not a cause.

### Hint: booking-v3 tells people to enter the wrong order

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form interprets the entry using the
day-first tenant locale. Copper Studio, Alder Physio and Pale Harbour have this form configuration.
Only their form-channel reports follow this mechanism. It needs a separate hint/form consistency fix;
changing only the shared parser does not repair the misleading label.

## How to reproduce the counts

Read `src/lib/data/reports-4471.json`, deduplicate by `id` with `uniqueById`, and select `mergedInto === 4471`.
Resolve each tenant from `src/lib/data/tenants/`, then run `causeOf` / `groupByCause` from `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`.
Date ordering is derived with `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, not guessed from browser locale or a manually typed country list.
Treat `meta.intakeTag` as an intake guess. Preserve the one-day descriptions in `desk-notes.json` as follow-up evidence.

The components calculate all report counts at render time. `npm run analyze` exports the exact
report-ID partition and prepares matching issue drafts; `npm run build` renders this SVX and its components.
Rendered files and issue bodies are snapshots and must be regenerated after source data changes.

## Why July looks like an incident spike

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged series measure different events. Reports were arriving in April,
well before the bulk merge in July. A jump in merges cannot date the start of the underlying defect.

## New triage rule

1. Resolve the tenant, its locale, its booking-form version and the report's channel; read the report body.
2. Compare failure mechanisms. The same subject can hide different causes; different subjects can describe the same cause.
3. Merge only when a shared cause is established. Unknown tenants and unclassified or contradictory symptoms require investigation.
4. Preserve the original report ID and dates. Never count an export overlap as a new customer report.
5. Keep the one-day/timing reports in their supplied group while requesting exact input, resulting timestamp,
   timezone, tenant and channel. Reclassify only with evidence, maintaining disjoint counts.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Follow-through and review

The separate issue drafts include counts, evidence IDs, mechanisms and acceptance checks.
After publication their links appear in the table above. Rebuild after updating `issue-links.json`.
Review this proposed decision with the support owner before replacing the operational rule.
Keep decision 0001 unchanged; record acceptance or further policy changes in a subsequent record.

